WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.820
- [Narrator] This is the USS Louisiana.

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:05.670
It's one of the Navy's
current 14 nuclear-powered

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:07.740
ballistic missile submarines.

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:11.580
This is China's version, the Type 094.

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:13.770
It's one of the nation's
six nuclear-powered

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:15.930
ballistic missile submarines.

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:18.510
These submarines serve the same purpose.

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:20.430
They are a sort of insurance policy

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:23.040
in the event of a nuclear attack.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.440
But to do its job, each
of these submarines

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:29.320
has to do one thing really well: hide.

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:30.450
(light music)

00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:33.780
We compare the Ohio class
submarines' technical capabilities

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:36.030
and missiles with the Jin classes

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:38.160
to see which comes out on top,

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:41.700
and take a look at what
each country has in store

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:43.620
for the future of these nuclear-powered

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:45.810
ballistic missile submarines.

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:46.830
(fanfare music)

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:50.700
On June 9th, 1959, a
decade into the Cold War,

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:53.370
the United States launched this submarine,

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:55.440
the USS George Washington.

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:57.600
It was the Navy's first nuclear-powered

00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:59.460
ballistic missile submarine.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:01.620
These SSBNs are nuclear-powered,

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:03.270
carrying nuclear missiles,

00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:07.260
and are one arm of the US
military's nuclear triad,

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:10.170
which includes intercontinental
ballistic missiles,

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:12.030
ballistic missile submarines,

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:13.890
and bombers.

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:17.370
And for the United States,
SSBNs became a crucial part

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:20.580
of its nuclear triad because
of their survivability.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:22.770
That is, their ability to remain safe

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:24.690
from anti-submarine warfare,

00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:29.220
and, if needed, launch
missiles past enemy defenses.

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:33.933
In fact, SSBNs are the most
survivable arm in the triad.

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:37.980
So in the 1980s, the first Ohio class

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:42.000
nuclear-powered ballistic missile
submarine entered service.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.580
The Ohio class, still active today,

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:48.090
was the Navy's sixth generation of SSBNs.

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:50.400
18 submarines in this class were built

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:52.410
over the next 16 years,

00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:53.880
though four were later converted

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:56.190
into cruise missile submarines.

00:01:56.190 --> 00:01:59.520
As these Ohio class SSBNs were procured,

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:02.550
China was building its first
ballistic missile submarine,

00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:07.290
the Type 092, which became
operational in 1983.

00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:10.170
They only fielded one, but
it marked a turning point

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:12.780
in China's nuclear capabilities.

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:15.240
A few decades later, in 2006,

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:19.950
satellite imagery spotted the
next generation, the Type 094.

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:22.200
In its 2022 annual report,

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:23.550
the Department of Defense says

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:26.610
China has six operational
Jin class submarines

00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:28.980
running deterrence patrols.

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:33.270
But these Type 094s are much
noisier than the 1970s era

00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:35.221
Soviet nuclear submarines.

00:02:35.221 --> 00:02:38.263
- And that would can safely say undermine

00:02:38.263 --> 00:02:42.057
the overall survivability
of the Chinese submarines,

00:02:42.057 --> 00:02:44.433
and therefore undermines
the overall credibility

00:02:44.433 --> 00:02:47.400
of a sea-based nuclear deterrence.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.290
- [Narrator] Dr. Tong
Zhao has been researching

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:52.980
China's nuclear capabilities
and strategic security issues

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:54.243
for about a decade.

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:57.900
The Ohio class submarines
are capable of carrying

00:02:57.900 --> 00:02:59.790
up to 24 missiles,

00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:01.950
but to comply with the New START treaty

00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:04.230
only 20 are allowed to be carried.

00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:06.510
In contrast, the Jin class submarines

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:08.853
can only carry up to 12 missiles.

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:12.300
And like each submarine survivability,

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:15.060
the missiles they carry
are also critical factors

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:17.370
in each military's strategy.

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:20.850
The Navy's SSBNs have held
a variety of missiles.

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:24.090
Today, the Ohio class
submarines carry this missile,

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:26.070
the Trident II.

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:29.640
It's 44-feet long, has
an 83-inch diameter,

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:32.220
and weighs 130,000 pounds.

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:35.043
That's about the weight
of five school buses.

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:39.030
Although the Trident II can
carry up to eight warheads,

00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:41.130
it likely carries only three or four

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:43.770
to comply with the New START treaty.

00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:45.840
But most importantly, it has a range

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:48.480
of about 4,000 nautical miles.

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:52.230
And since 2017, the Navy has
been updating these missiles

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:56.040
to extend their life and
increase their range even more

00:03:56.040 --> 00:04:00.120
to about 6,480 nautical miles.

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:01.560
The Department of Defense says

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:05.100
that China's Jin class
submarines carry either the JL-2

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:08.910
or its next iteration, the JL-3.

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:10.920
The JL-2 has one warhead,

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:12.843
while the JL-3 has multiple.

00:04:14.461 --> 00:04:16.650
The JL-2's range is estimated to be about

00:04:16.650 --> 00:04:19.590
3,780 nautical miles,

00:04:19.590 --> 00:04:24.333
and the JL-3's range reaches
at least 5,400 nautical miles.

00:04:25.170 --> 00:04:27.330
This means that from China's shores,

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:31.890
the JL-2 has sufficient range
to target Guam or Alaska.

00:04:31.890 --> 00:04:35.340
But the JL-3 might
target the continental US

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:37.530
from areas near China's coast.

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:39.330
But,
- Even if you put

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:42.180
a longer range missiles on
the Jin class submarines,

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:44.100
it's still the Jin class
submarines. (chuckles)

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:46.170
They're still as noisy as the old ones.

00:04:46.170 --> 00:04:48.480
- [Narrator] Hans Kristensen
has studied the status

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:51.870
of American nuclear
weapons since the 1980s.

00:04:51.870 --> 00:04:55.470
And since noisiness impacts
an SSBNs survivability,

00:04:55.470 --> 00:04:57.480
it makes it harder for China's submarines

00:04:57.480 --> 00:05:00.750
to travel further from the
shores without being detected.

00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:02.190
- Even if you get longer range,

00:05:02.190 --> 00:05:04.200
that range is not sufficient to cover

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:05.820
all of the continental United States,

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:08.220
unless you sail far into the Pacific.

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:09.570
- [Narrator] And that's also difficult

00:05:09.570 --> 00:05:11.370
for China's submarines to do

00:05:11.370 --> 00:05:13.740
because of US anti-submarine defenses

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:15.480
across the Pacific Ocean.

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:18.990
- A sea-based nuclear
capabilities is one area

00:05:18.990 --> 00:05:23.340
in which China still lags
behind the United States

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:25.110
in a very significant manner.

00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:28.120
China understands that eventually

00:05:29.010 --> 00:05:32.370
nuclear submarine may be
the most survivable part

00:05:32.370 --> 00:05:35.040
of a country's nuclear arsenal.

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:37.560
So China had this long-term vision

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:39.930
to eventually acquire very advanced

00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:42.120
nuclear submarine technologies.

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:45.510
- [Narrator] So what's China
working on to close the gap?

00:05:45.510 --> 00:05:47.580
China is expected to begin construction

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:52.170
on its next generation
of SSBN, the Type 096,

00:05:52.170 --> 00:05:54.630
in the first part of this decade.

00:05:54.630 --> 00:05:58.080
These submarines will run with
the existing Jin class ones,

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:01.500
and experts like Dr. Zhao
say that the Type 096

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:04.440
will be quieter than its predecessor.

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:06.810
But the US is forging ahead, too.

00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:09.630
Construction on the
next generation of SSBNs

00:06:09.630 --> 00:06:11.610
is already underway.

00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:14.220
These Columbia class
boomers will be even bigger

00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:15.603
than their predecessors.

00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:18.667
- This thing about the
modernization cycle,

00:06:18.667 --> 00:06:21.030
you know, they don't
happen at the same time

00:06:21.030 --> 00:06:23.550
in China and the United States.

00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:26.220
So there's sometimes sort
of a simplistic tendency

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:28.590
or a temptation in the debate

00:06:28.590 --> 00:06:30.787
for nuclear advocates to say,

00:06:30.787 --> 00:06:33.990
"See, they're modernizing, we're not.

00:06:33.990 --> 00:06:35.760
Therefore, we're behind."

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:36.990
But now we have actually,

00:06:36.990 --> 00:06:39.490
now we're entering our modernization cycle

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:45.150
and that will run for the
next two to three decades.

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:46.470
- [Narrator] The Ohio class submarines

00:06:46.470 --> 00:06:48.660
are reaching the end of their lifetime.

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:51.420
In just a few years, the
first of its generation,

00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:54.360
the USS Ohio, will retire.

00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:58.980
By 2040 they are set to be
replaced by Columbia class SSBNs.

00:06:58.980 --> 00:07:01.890
The Navy is acquiring 12 new SSBNs

00:07:01.890 --> 00:07:05.190
and each one will have
room for 16 missiles,

00:07:05.190 --> 00:07:07.860
eight less than the
Ohio class could carry.

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:10.230
Cutting back might seem counterintuitive,

00:07:10.230 --> 00:07:12.900
but the US military
estimates no more are needed

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:15.480
to meet nuclear deterrence requirements.

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:18.000
And the US Navy says the
Columbia class submarines

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:19.620
will be even quieter.

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:20.970
That's because they're being built

00:07:20.970 --> 00:07:23.370
with an electric drive propulsion train

00:07:23.370 --> 00:07:26.220
versus the traditional
mechanical drive system.

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:29.760
So even if China's Type
096 is considerably quieter

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:31.230
than its predecessor,

00:07:31.230 --> 00:07:32.940
it could be far less stealthy

00:07:32.940 --> 00:07:35.910
than the US' upcoming Columbia class.

00:07:35.910 --> 00:07:39.093
- So we may see some
incremental improvements.

00:07:40.005 --> 00:07:42.660
That doesn't mean that China will be

00:07:42.660 --> 00:07:44.610
at the same technical level

00:07:44.610 --> 00:07:46.905
with this American counterpart.

00:07:46.905 --> 00:07:49.405
(light music)

